<Base title="About Kristen" description="Meet Kristen Dean, a former family caregiver and certified caregiver who supports people caring for loved ones with dementia and Alzheimer's.">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <header class="page-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>Meet Kristen</h1>
    </header>
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="lead">Kristen Dean knows caregiving from the inside. For about five years, she cared for her grandma, who had Alzheimer's disease.</p>
      <p>Kristen was only 18 or 19 when she moved into her grandma's retirement home to look after her. She lived there through the good days and the hard ones, learning firsthand how much love, patience, and practical know-how it takes to care for someone whose memory is slipping away.</p>
      <p>Since then, Kristen has earned several caregiving certificates, including a national caregiver certification, and she is working toward her Washington State certification.</p>

      <h2>Why Caring for My Caregivers</h2>
      <p>Kristen started Caring for My Caregivers for the people who so often get overlooked: family members and close friends caring for someone with dementia or Alzheimer's, whether they are paid or unpaid. These caregivers give so much of themselves, and they often get very little support in return. Many struggle with exhaustion, isolation, and depression, and too many reach a breaking point.</p>
      <p>Here, Kristen shares:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Encouragement</strong> for the hard days, from someone who has lived them.</li>
        <li><strong>Practical tips and activity ideas</strong> for connecting with a loved one at every stage of the disease.</li>
        <li><strong>Resource information</strong>, because so many families don't know how much help is available.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>She also created the adventures of <a href="/stories">Grandma Rose and Lily</a>, gentle stories that help parents explain memory loss to their children, and wrote the picture book <em>Grandma Remembers Jesus: A Gentle Story of Alzheimer's, Family, and God's Unforgettable Love</em>.</p>

      <div class="note">
        <p><strong>If you are a caregiver and you're struggling, please reach out.</strong> You matter too. In the U.S., call or text <strong>988</strong> any time to reach the Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline, or call the Alzheimer's Association Helpline at <strong>1-800-272-3900</strong>.</p>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:1.5em"><a class="btn" href="/contact">Get in touch with Kristen</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
